Cognitive ergonomics is the design of information, tools, environments, and interfaces around the way human cognition actually works—attention, memory, perception, decision-making, error, workload, and mental models.

In the frame we’ve been using, it’s basically:

Don’t just ask whether an idea is true. Ask whether the mind can comfortably carry it.

A doctrine, interface, warning, story, or system can be logically sound and still be cognitively hostile if it is introduced in the wrong order, at the wrong resolution, with the wrong metaphor, or under too much emotional load.

That gives you a useful Pantheon-style principle:

semantic correctness ≠ cognitive usability

And another:

placement is part of meaning.

The same proposition can produce radically different internal models depending on when and how it is encountered.

That’s cognitive ergonomics applied to theology, storytelling, software, education—pretty much everything involving minds.